  "notes": "Whether it stays after 2026 not stated. Drawn from April 2026 photos of N1776R (left side). Not the Heart fin: navy, white and red bands on the same lines as the Heart's. The right side is not seen in these photos. Re-checked 2026-11-15 on the April 2026 broadside photo (doors as the scale: 45 px/m): the quotation's two lines had no length and ran far too long; measured, 'Life, Liberty and the' runs 21.9-29.3 m back (x 0.575-0.771) above the windows, 'pursuit of Happiness.' 24.6-31.0 m (0.646-0.816) just under them, the script's lowercase about 0.45 of its capitals. '1776' checked (6.3-12.4 m, as drawn). The red sweep and the cream under it, traced as points along curves, made smooth. The fin's bands put on the Heart fin's lines as re-measured on Colorado One (the white where the Heart's red is, the red where its yellow is), with the same 'Southwest' title; drawn on the 737-8. 2026-11-15: a Commons photo whose source is airliners.net was dropped (the dataset doesn't draw from photo sites); its parts now cite the other photo. Re-checked 2026-11-27 on the BWI photos (2026-04-30) at full resolution: the '250' under the cockpit is about 0.5 m tall, not 1 m (height 0.2, was 0.4), with 'AMERICA' small over it; the thirteen fuselage stars measured one by one against the overwing exits and the rear door (MD3); the cowling's star ring spans about two thirds of its height (1.35, was 1.2). 2026-11-28, on the owner's review: '1776' and the two lines of the quotation are drawn as marks (SWA/script-1776, SWA/script-life-liberty, SWA/script-pursuit), the lettering traced off the BWI photo (MD1) rather than set in a stand-in script. Their places were re-measured on it against the forward and aft doors and the window row. 'Life, Liberty and the' runs x 0.599-0.753 just over the windows; it had been drawn from x 0.575, too far forward and too long. 'pursuit of Happiness.' runs 0.637-0.794 under them, and '1776' runs 0.154-0.314. Lettering isn't mirrored. The right side isn't in any open photo."
